<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Ligdan Khan</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1588">1588</a>–<a href="/years/1634">1634</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/17th-century">17th century</a> · Of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/borjigin">Borjigin</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Ligdan Khan was the last great khan of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/northern-yuan">Northern Yuan</a>, ruling the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chahar-mongols">Chahar Mongols</a> from 1604. He tried to restore central authority over the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mongols">Mongols</a> by force and patronised <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tibetan-buddhism">Tibetan Buddhism</a>, sponsoring a Mongolian translation of the Kanjur. Defeated by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manchus">Manchu</a> Later Jin, he fled west and died in 1634, and his son surrendered the following year.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ligdan-khan.jpg" alt="Ligdan Khan" width="320" height="427" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Avishay · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:LIGDEN_HUTAGT_KHAN.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Ligdan, born in 1588, was a son of Mangghus Mergen Taiji and a grandson of Buyan Sechen Khan. Because his father died early, he was chosen to succeed his grandfather as khan of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/northern-yuan">Northern Yuan</a>, with the reign title Khutugtu. His name comes from the Classical Tibetan legs-ldan; western scholars usually write Ligdan and Mongolian scholars Ligden, and in Chinese it is written Lindan. The khan's own <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/appanage">appanage</a>, the Chakhar, then lived in the Sira Mören valley.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>Ligdan divided the Chakhar into right and left wings and built Chaghan city near Abaga Khara Mountain. In his early years other tümens respected him, and with the Baarin and Jarud princes of the Southern Khalkha he raided the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ming-empire">Ming dynasty</a>. Seeking to strengthen his hold, he moved the Buddhist religious centre of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mongols">Mongols</a> to Chakhar, revived the Saskyapa order of Kublai's time and in 1617 made its Sharba pandita his preceptor; he also built temples at Küriye.</p><p>In 1618 he agreed with the Ming to guard their northern border against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manchus">Manchus</a> in return for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silver">silver</a>, an annual subsidy of 40,000 taels by 1620. He broke with the Later Jin ruler <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nurhaci">Nurhaci</a> in 1620, and most of the Eastern Tümens went over to Nurhaci in 1622–24. His centralising rule, with officials over the wings, a court nobility and a corps of 300 baaturs, drove more tribes to the Manchus. In 1627 the Sunid, Uzemchin and Abaga princes and the Three Right Wing Tumens attacked him at Zhaocheng; he won but lost 10,000 Chakhars. Raids on the Ming then raised his subsidy to 81,000 taels.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>In 1632 the Later Jin khan <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hong-taiji">Hong Taiji</a> and his Mongol allies marched against Ligdan, who avoided battle and fled with perhaps 100,000 Chakhar to Kokenuur. Allied with the Tibetan monarchs, he opposed Dalai Lama V and Banchin Erdene IV. He died of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/smallpox">smallpox</a> at Sira Tala, in present-day <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gansu">Gansu</a>, in 1634 while marching to attack the dGe-lugs-pa order in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tibet">Tibet</a>. His son Ejei Khan was handed over to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qing-empire">Qing dynasty</a>, which soon established control over <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/inner-mongolia">Inner Mongolia</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Ligdan Khan” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote><b>Ligdan Khan</b>, the last great khan, tried to restore central power but was defeated by the Manchus and died in 1634.</blockquote><a href="/state/northern-yuan">Northern Yuan</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Ligdan Khan</b> of the Chahar overran their lands and Guihua in 1627 to 1628; when Hong Taiji's campaign of 1632 drove him west, the Tümed soon submitted to the Later Jin (1616-1636).</blockquote><a href="/state/tumed-altan-khan">Tumed (Altan Khan)</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mongolia">Mongolia</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gansu">Gansu</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/china">China</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=46,105&amp;year=1617" aria-label="Mongolia on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of Ligdan Khan"><image href="/maps/globes/g120n60.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4119 6085h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4119 6085h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=46,105&amp;year=1617">What was here in 1617? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
